(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-exam-2-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient with streptococcal pharyngitis is penicillin-allergic. Which agents are offered?","choices":["Cefdinir, cefixime or ceftriaxone","Levofloxacin or moxifloxacin","Cephalexin, clindamycin or azithromycin","Doxycycline or trimethoprim"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three alternatives rather than one, which is worth holding onto because the allergy lists differ between the ear, the sinuses and the throat. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which statement about serious liver injury from statins is accurate?","choices":["It affects a quarter of users","It is exceedingly rare","It is the main reason to stop","It occurs regardless of dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Liver enzyme rises occur in 0.5 to 2.5% of patients, dose-dependently, but serious liver problems are exceedingly rare. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"At diagnosis, a patient's systolic pressure is 24 mmHg above goal. Which starting regimen is recommended?","choices":["ACE inhibitor plus a receptor blocker","ACE inhibitor plus a dihydropyridine","Beta blocker plus clonidine","A dihydropyridine by itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When systolic pressure is more than 20 mmHg (or diastolic more than 10 mmHg) above goal, start an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker plus a dihydropyridine. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 38-year-old reports a foreign body sensation after gardening. Which agent reveals an epithelial defect?","choices":["Tropicamide","Fluorescein","Phenylephrine","Proparacaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it discloses corneal injury and stains the anterior segment. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"An older patient on verapamil has a heart rate of 48 beats per minute. Which other adverse effects follow from the same cardiac action?","choices":["Reflex tachycardia and palpitations","Hypertrichosis and pericardial effusion","First-degree block and worse heart failure","Dry cough and angioedema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Negative inotropic and nodal effects produce first-degree atrioventricular block, bradycardia, and exacerbation of heart failure or pulmonary edema. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to diltiazem?","choices":["Supraventricular tachycardia","Chronic stable angina","Uncomplicated hypertension","Advanced heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Advanced heart block and hypotension are contraindications; heart failure, liver disease and gastroesophageal reflux disease are relative contraindications. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which apolipoprotein binds LDL (low-density lipoprotein) to its receptor?","choices":["ApoA-I","ApoC-II","ApoE","ApoB-100"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ApoB-100 is the only apoprotein of LDL and the ligand for the LDL receptor, which clears about 75% of LDL particles, mostly in the liver. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which beta-1 selective blocker is given intravenously?","choices":["Atenolol","Bisoprolol","Acebutolol","Esmolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Esmolol is the second-generation, beta-1 selective agent marked for intravenous use. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which combination is a relative contraindication for statin therapy?","choices":["A statin with ezetimibe","A statin with lisinopril","A statin with gemfibrozil","A statin with amlodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Relative contraindications are concomitant cyclosporine or other immunosuppressants, gemfibrozil, niacin and erythromycin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 42-year-old with no diabetes and no cardiovascular disease has an LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol of 212 mg/dL. Does he fall into a statin benefit group?","choices":["No, he needs diabetes as well","No, he must be at least 55","No, he needs prior heart disease","Yes, LDL above 190 alone qualifies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Individuals with LDL above 190 mg/dL form one of the four statin benefit groups, whatever their other risk factors. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which ocular antivirals are given by mouth, and for which conditions?","choices":["Oral natamycin for fungal keratitis","Oral foscarnet for cytomegalovirus retinitis","Oral trifluridine for herpes simplex keratoconjunctivitis","Acyclovir, valacyclovir or famciclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The systemic agents handle zoster ophthalmicus and simplex keratitis or iridocyclitis, while trifluridine and ganciclovir are the topical options. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is a common side effect of statins?","choices":["Headache and sleep disturbance","Cutaneous flushing and itching","Constipation and bloating","Gallstones and abdominal pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Common statin side effects are headache, sleep disturbance, fatigue, gastrointestinal intolerance, flu-like symptoms and liver enzyme rises. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which enzyme's interactions call for caution with calcium channel blockers?","choices":["Cytochrome P450 3A4","Cytochrome P450 2D6","Cytochrome P450 2C9","Cytochrome P450 1A2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers are subject to cytochrome P450 3A4 interactions, so drugs that inhibit or induce it change their levels. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 21-year-old contact lens wearer is treated for conjunctivitis. When may she resume her lenses?","choices":["Immediately, as long as she is using drops","After finishing the full antibiotic course, regardless of the eye","After 7 days in all cases","When the eye is not inflamed and there has been no discharge for 24 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and she should get rid of her eye makeup too. With the lens discarded or disinfected. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What adverse effects are described with pseudoephedrine?","choices":["Sedation and dry mouth","Diarrhea and abdominal pain","Fast heart rate","Nosebleed and bitter taste"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three follow from stimulating alpha receptors throughout the body rather than only in the nose. Raised blood pressure and headache. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How are long-acting nitrates usually used?","choices":["As first-line monotherapy","As add-on therapy","Only for acute attacks","Only after an infarction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are usually adjunctive, added when a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker is not enough; monotherapy is not recommended. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How does trifluridine interfere with viral replication?","choices":["It competitively inhibits deoxyguanosine triphosphate binding to DNA polymerase","It inhibits viral neuraminidase","It blocks viral attachment to the corneal epithelium","It inhibits thymidylate synthetase and is incorporated into viral DNA in place of thymidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It both starves the virus of thymidine and substitutes itself into the growing strand — two hits on the same pathway. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why do non-dihydropyridines slow atrioventricular conduction while dihydropyridines do not?","choices":["They block T-type channels only","They also block sodium channels","They also block beta-1 receptors","They also delay slow-channel recovery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both reduce the slow inward calcium current, but only the non-dihydropyridines also decrease the channel's rate of recovery, which slows atrioventricular conduction. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has substernal pressure whenever he climbs two flights of stairs, relieved by a few minutes of rest. This pattern has been unchanged in frequency and severity for two years, with no rest pain. The resting electrocardiogram is normal and cardiac biomarkers are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic stable angina","Unstable angina","Variant angina","Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exertional pressure that is relieved by rest, has been stable for two years and comes with normal biomarkers is chronic stable angina, graded by the activity that provokes it. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is an angiotensin receptor blocker?","choices":["Benazepril","Valsartan","Nisoldipine","Nadolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The -sartan suffix identifies an angiotensin receptor blocker, the same family as losartan, candesartan, irbesartan and telmisartan. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A child has a fever during chickenpox. Why must aspirin be avoided?","choices":["It raises the risk of Reye's syndrome","It is ineffective against viral fever","It causes irreversible hearing loss in children","It interacts with the varicella vaccine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A viral illness plus aspirin in a child is the exact combination that produces it, which is why the rule is about the setting rather than the drug alone. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why are younger patients usually intolerant of miotic therapy?","choices":["Systemic bradycardia from muscarinic activation","Permanent iris color change","Visual blurring from induced myopia","Increased airway resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fixed small pupils, induced myopia and visual disturbance are the effects, and a young eye with active accommodation notices them most. That, plus frequent dosing, is why compliance is poor. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is ketoconazole indicated for here?","choices":["Oral candidiasis alone","Fungal otitis externa","Prophylaxis during antibiotic courses","Systemic fungal infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the contrast with nystatin is the point: one treats infection throughout the body, the other never leaves the mouth and gut. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How do beta blockers relieve benign essential tremor?","choices":["Cardiac beta-1 blockade","Vascular alpha-1 blockade","Skeletal muscle beta-2 blockade","Central alpha-2 stimulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blocking beta-2 receptors on skeletal muscle is the mechanism given for benign essential tremor; panic attacks respond through sympathetic and central effects. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"When should a patient starting an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor be most alert for angioedema?","choices":["During the first week","After six months or more","Only after a dose increase","Only after stopping the drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angioedema usually develops in the first week of therapy and is reversible if the drug is removed. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How do beta blockers lower angiotensin II?","choices":["Inhibiting the converting enzyme","Blocking the AT1 receptor","Blocking renal beta-1 receptors","Blocking lung beta-2 receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta-1 receptors on the juxtaglomerular cells mediate renin release, so blocking them reduces renin and therefore angiotensin II. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Why is more than one agent often needed to control intraocular pressure?","choices":["Targeting production and outflow together","Tolerance develops to every single agent within weeks","Each agent can only be used at half dose","Single agents are contraindicated in open angle disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Combinations are described as synergistic for exactly that reason, and they also cut the number of drops a patient has to manage, which helps compliance. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 64 and 73"},{"q":"How does niacin reduce hepatic VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) production?","choices":["More fatty acid oxidation","Binding of bile acids","Less fatty acid mobilized from fat","Blocking cholesterol uptake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Niacin decreases mobilization of free fatty acids from adipose tissue, so the liver makes less triglyceride, VLDL and apo B; LDL falls and HDL rises. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which adverse effects are listed for the lovastatin and extended-release niacin combination?","choices":["Cough, angioedema and hyperkalemia","Hepatotoxicity, myopathy and flushing","Bloating, constipation and flatulence","Gallstones, nausea and diarrhea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The combination carries the statin's muscle and liver risks and niacin's flushing. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the loss of nitrate effect with continuous use called?","choices":["Anaphylaxis","Reflex tachycardia","Tachyphylaxis","Rebound angina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tachyphylaxis is rapid tolerance to a nitrate's effect, handled with a nitrate-free interval. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Where is the cough reflex organized?","choices":["In the cerebral cortex, under voluntary control","In the cervical spinal cord","In a cough center in the medulla oblongata","In the vagal ganglia alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Receptors in the airway start it, but the medulla organizes it, which is why one drug class works at each end. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Besides fundoscopy, what therapeutic use do cycloplegics have?","choices":["Glaucoma, to increase aqueous outflow","Bacterial conjunctivitis, to reduce photophobia","Uveitis, to prevent synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm","Dry eye, to reduce tear evaporation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Keeping the pupil moving stops the iris adhering, and relaxing the ciliary muscle relieves the pain of spasm. So they are not purely diagnostic agents. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which is a listed therapeutic use of calcium channel blockers as a class?","choices":["Migraine prophylaxis","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Hypertensive crisis by infusion","Benign essential tremor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class uses are angina, hypertension, supraventricular arrhythmias, diastolic heart failure, cerebral ischemia and migraine prophylaxis. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"For which lipid pattern is niacin particularly useful?","choices":["Atherogenic dyslipidemia","Isolated high HDL","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Pregnancy-related high LDL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Niacin is useful for atherogenic dyslipidemia and as combination therapy when atherogenic dyslipidemia comes with elevated LDL (low-density lipoprotein). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the major central nervous system side effect of first generation H1 antagonists?","choices":["Tremor","Headache","Seizures at ordinary use","Sedation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the dominant side effect, and it is also the basis of an entire over-the-counter market in sleep aids. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A patient on a diuretic and an ACE inhibitor starts taking ibuprofen regularly. What should be watched?","choices":["Renal function","Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Thyroid function","Serum potassium only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two interactions at once: the kidney is at risk with the diuretic, and the ACE inhibitor works less well. The blood pressure response to the ACE inhibitor. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Ibuprofen is avoided below a certain age. Which group?","choices":["All children under twelve","Young infants","All children under fifteen, because of Reye's syndrome","Adolescents only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There is a lower age limit below which it is not used, which distinguishes it from acetaminophen. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A toddler swallows a bottle of an imidazoline eye drop. Why does an agent that vasoconstricts locally cause central nervous system depression when ingested?","choices":["It acts on alpha-1 receptors both locally and systemically","It is metabolized to an opioid-like compound in the gut","Locally it acts on alpha-1 receptors, but systemically it targets alpha-2","It blocks rather than stimulates adrenergic receptors when absorbed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That split is the whole reason the question is asked. Alpha-1 activation in the conjunctival vessels gives the local vasoconstriction; absorbed systemically the drug acts at alpha-2 receptors, and central alpha-2 agonism produces sedation, bradycardia and depressed respiration. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is ezetimibe's effect on HDL (high-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["A large rise","Insignificant","A sharp fall","A rise greater than niacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe lowers LDL (low-density lipoprotein) modestly and triglycerides slightly, with an insignificant effect on HDL. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 45-year-old needs pupil dilation for fundoscopy, and you want the pupil to stay as reactive to light as possible. Which agent?","choices":["Atropine","Phenylephrine","Tropicamide","Cyclopentolate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The sympathomimetic leaves the pupil more reactive to light than an antimuscarinic does. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which item in a patient's diet interacts with simvastatin through CYP450 metabolism?","choices":["Salt substitutes","Alcohol","Grapefruit juice","Antacids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grapefruit juice is listed with verapamil, amiodarone, niacin and fibrates as a statin interaction, especially involving CYP3A4. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of ocular nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs matters most in a patient who also has glaucoma?","choices":["Increased tear production","A rise in intraocular pressure","Rebound hyperemia","Cataract formation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lacrimation, keratitis and irritation are the nuisance effects; the pressure rise is the one that interacts with an existing diagnosis and could undo glaucoma control. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What three actions does ibuprofen have?","choices":["Anti-inflammatory, analgesic and antipyretic","Analgesic and antipyretic only","Anti-inflammatory and antiplatelet only","Antipyretic only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three at ordinary use, unlike aspirin where they separate out by exposure. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is clonidine often given with a diuretic?","choices":["It causes sodium retention","It causes hyperkalemia","It raises renin sharply","It causes rebound edema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine causes sodium retention, so it is often given with a diuretic; it also decreases antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin) secretion. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which effect belongs to H2 rather than H1 receptors?","choices":["Stimulation of sensory nerve endings","Increased post-capillary permeability","Bronchoconstriction","Increased gastric acid secretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the reason H2 blockers are stomach drugs while H1 blockers are allergy drugs. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which is a pleiotropic effect of statins, beyond lowering cholesterol?","choices":["Plaque stabilization","Raised triglycerides","Increased platelet stickiness","Increased inflammatory markers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pleiotropic effects include plaque stabilization, improved endothelial function, platelet inhibition, reduced leukocyte adhesiveness and reduced inflammatory markers. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient on prazosin begins daily naproxen. What effect do NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) have on prazosin?","choices":["They enhance its hypotension","They attenuate its response","They raise its blood levels","They have no interaction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. NSAIDs attenuate the response to prazosin, as they also reduce the effect of ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Why might glaucoma therapy be started in one eye only?","choices":["Because bilateral treatment doubles the systemic absorption to unsafe levels","Because only the worse eye requires treatment","To judge efficacy and tolerability against the untreated eye","To reduce the cost of therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The other eye acts as its own control, which tells you whether the drug is doing anything and whether the patient can tolerate it before both eyes are committed. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man presents two hours after the onset of crushing chest pain. The electrocardiogram shows ST-segment elevation in the anterior leads. He has no bleeding history, no prior stroke and no recent surgery. Which drug restores coronary flow by dissolving the clot?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Clopidogrel","Nitroglycerin","Alteplase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A fibrinolytic activates plasminogen bound to fibrin, dissolving the clot in an early ST-elevation myocardial infarction with no contraindication. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 33-year-old asks for a prescription for the numbing drops that helped during foreign body removal. What do you do?","choices":["Decline — not for home use","Prescribe a short course for comfort","Prescribe them with a warning not to drive","Substitute a topical steroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The eye stays numb for 10 to 20 minutes with no blink reflex, which is unsafe unsupervised. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which warning accompanies the list of nasal corticosteroids?","choices":["They must not be used for longer than a few days","Many have inhaled versions for asthma","They are all prescription only","They cannot be used alongside an antihistamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The example given is fluticasone: the nasal product and the asthma inhaler are different preparations of the same drug with different names. The names are easy to confuse. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 40-year-old is shown how to use both a drop and an ointment. Why must she cover the lacrimal duct after the DROP but not the ointment?","choices":["The ointment is too viscous to drain away","The ointment is absorbed entirely through the cornea","Ointments have no systemic absorption at all","Drops are sterile and ointments are not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a drop, occluding the duct limits nasolacrimal drainage and the systemic absorption that follows it. So there is nothing to block. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A patient in her third trimester cannot take an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor. Is an angiotensin receptor blocker an acceptable substitute?","choices":["No, it is also avoided then","Yes, it spares the fetus","Yes, at a reduced dose","Only if potassium is normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers share the fetal morbidity and mortality and are not given in the second and third trimesters. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which condition is listed as a contraindication to statins?","choices":["Type 2 diabetes","Hypertension","Prior stroke","Hepatic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic disease and pregnancy are the listed contraindications; liver enzyme rises on therapy are managed by reducing the dose or pausing. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"When is a patient on guaifenesin told to follow up?","choices":["Only if a fever develops","If symptoms persist beyond about a week or get worse","After a single day without improvement","Never -- it is available without prescription"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A cough that outlasts the self-treatment window needs a cause found rather than more expectorant. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How does the ACC/AHA (American College of Cardiology/American Heart Association) cholesterol guideline differ from ATP III (Adult Treatment Panel III)?","choices":["It targets an LDL below 100 mg/dL","It does not treat to an LDL target","It starts everyone on high intensity","It makes niacin the first choice"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It starts moderate- or high-intensity statin therapy for the four benefit groups and does not titrate to a specific LDL (low-density lipoprotein) target. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A child treated for acute otitis media has not improved after three days, and has had no antibiotics in the past three months. What is the next step?","choices":["Continue the same agent for a further three days","Move straight to injected ceftriaxone","Stop antibiotics and observe","Amoxicillin-clavulanate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Failure at three days is the trigger to step up, and this is the step. Or a third generation cephalosporin. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which findings should be checked before and after a beta blocker is given in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Body temperature and weight","Serum sodium and calcium","Heart rate and blood pressure","Urine output and appetite"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cautions are bradycardia and hypotension, so heart rate and blood pressure are watched closely. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 36-year-old has a small peripheral corneal infection. Why can a topical antibiotic reach concentrations at the eye that an intravenous one cannot?","choices":["The cornea actively transports antibiotics","It gives very high LOCAL concentrations","Topical drugs avoid the blood-brain barrier","Topical drugs have greater systemic bioavailability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and the trade-off is that more involved infection still needs systemic therapy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which bleeding history contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["Occasional spotting from hemorrhoids","A nosebleed during childhood","Bruising after a minor fall","Recent serious gastrointestinal bleeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Serious gastrointestinal bleeding in the recent past is a listed contraindication, since fibrinolytics could restart serious bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"}]};
